WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
This lesson walks through calibration, verifying that a model's confidence scores actually match its real accuracy.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:14.000
We'll cover what calibration means, how to build a labeled validation set, how to run the calibration check,

3
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.000
and how to read a reliability diagram to spot miscalibration.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Let's get into it.

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.000
A self-reported number is only worth what it predicts.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Calibration is the check that decides whether a model's confidence scores predict anything at all.

7
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:37.000
A validation set is a held-out collection of labeled samples you use to measure how accurate the extractions really are.

8
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:43.000
A reliability diagram plots what the model claims against what it actually delivers.

9
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.000
So miscalibration becomes something you can see, rather than something you suspect.

10
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:55.000
And the routing threshold is the cutoff you set from that measured result, instead of from raw model output.

11
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:02.000
Each one depends on the one before it, and together they turn a self-reported score into evidence you can route on.

12
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Calibration has a specific meaning worth stating precisely.

13
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.000
A calibrated model is one where the score it reports matches its actual accuracy.

14
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:19.000
If the model says 0.9 on a field type, a calibrated model is correct on that field type 90% of the time.

15
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.000
That alignment is what you're checking for.

16
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.000
An overconfident model says 0.9, but is only correct 60% of the time.

17
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.000
It thinks it's doing well, but it's feeding errors into your auto-accept pipeline without any flag.

18
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.000
An underconfident model says 0.6, but is actually correct 90% of the time.

19
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:44.000
That wastes reviewer capacity on work that didn't need checking.

20
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.372
Both miscalibrations are worth fixing, but overconfidence is the more dangerous failure, because it

21
00:01:49.372 --> 00:01:50.945
silently lets errors through.

22
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Building a useful validation set requires three things.

23
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:03.000
First, the samples have to be representative of your actual production documents, not cherry-picked easy cases.

24
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:09.211
If your production data includes handwritten forms, scanned invoices, and digital submissions, the

25
00:02:09.211 --> 00:02:11.936
validation set should include all of those.

26
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:18.000
Second, every sample needs a ground truth label for each field you are measuring.

27
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.000
A human expert creates those labels by checking the correct value against the source document.

28
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.000
Third, the set stays held out.

29
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.972
You never use it to train the model or adjust the prompt, because that would contaminate the

30
00:02:30.972 --> 00:02:33.945
measurement and make the calibration result unreliable.

31
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:41.045
The contrast between an overconfident and a well-calibrated model shows up clearly in what happens

32
00:02:41.045 --> 00:02:44.928
downstream, and it's worth walking through both cases.

33
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:51.000
When the model is overconfident, it reports high scores on fields, it frequently gets wrong.

34
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:57.508
The system trusts those scores, auto-accepts the fields, and errors flow into production without

35
00:02:57.508 --> 00:02:58.932
anyone catching them.

36
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:04.000
The system looks efficient on paper, but is silently degrading output quality.

37
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:12.000
When the model is well-calibrated, a field scored at 0.85 is correct, roughly 85% of the time.

38
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:20.000
The acceptance threshold you set at 0.9 actually corresponds to 90% accuracy in practice.

39
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:27.000
That alignment is what makes per-field triage genuinely reliable, not just a mechanism for moving work around.

40
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.000
Running the calibration check is a four-step process.

41
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.000
Start by grouping the validation predictions into buckets, based on their confidence scores.

42
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:44.000
A bucket for 0.0 to 0.1, another for 0.1 to 0.2, and so on up to 1.0.

43
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:50.000
Then for each bucket, measure the fraction of predictions in that range that match the ground truth label.

44
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:55.000
That fraction is the empirical accuracy for that confidence range.

45
00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:03.000
Next, plot each bucket's midpoint confidence on the x-axis against its empirical accuracy on the y-axis.

46
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:07.000
Draw a diagonal line from the bottom left corner to the top right.

47
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.000
The diagonal represents perfect calibration.

48
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.000
Finally, find the gaps.

49
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:18.000
Bins below the diagonal indicate overconfidence, and bins above show underconfidence.

50
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:24.485
The distance from your plotted points to the diagonal tells you how miscalibrated the model is and

51
00:04:24.485 --> 00:04:26.933
where threshold adjustment is needed.

52
00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:35.000
The reliability diagram is the primary tool for communicating calibration results to anyone who sets routing policy.

53
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:40.000
The x-axis runs from 0 to 1.0 and shows the model's reported confidence.

54
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:47.000
The y-axis runs from 0 to 1.0 and shows the empirical accuracy measured on the validation set.

55
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:57.000
A perfectly calibrated model traces the diagonal exactly, so confidence of 0.7 maps to accuracy of 0.7.

56
00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:03.000
When the plotted line curves above the diagonal, the model is underconfident in that range.

57
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.000
When it curves below, the model is overconfident.

58
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:12.211
A diagram that hugs the diagonal across all confidence ranges tells you the scores are trustworthy

59
00:05:12.211 --> 00:05:14.936
enough to drive routing decisions directly.

60
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:20.000
Once you have the reliability diagram, adjusting the routing threshold is straightforward.

61
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.000
Start with your policy's accuracy requirement.

62
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:30.245
If the business needs at least 95% accuracy on auto-accepted fields, find the 0.95 level on the

63
00:05:30.245 --> 00:05:31.923
y-axis of the diagram.

64
00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:37.000
Trace horizontally to where your calibration curve crosses that accuracy level.

65
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:42.000
Then read down to the x-axis to find the corresponding confidence value.

66
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.000
Set that confidence value as your routing threshold.

67
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:52.000
Fields scoring at or above it will achieve roughly 95% accuracy based on the calibration data.

68
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:54.000
Fields below it go to review.

69
00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:00.000
The threshold now reflects real performance rather than the model's self-assessment.

70
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.000
Calibration results don't stay valid indefinitely.

71
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:08.139
When a team upgrades to a newer model version, the confidence distribution shifts because the new

72
00:06:08.139 --> 00:06:10.947
model generates different scores for the same inputs.

73
00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:19.000
When a prompt is revised, even a subtle change to phrasing or examples can move scores across the threshold boundary.

74
00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:24.000
Both situations require rerunning the calibration check on the same validation set.

75
00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:28.000
The validation set itself stays fixed, so the comparison is fair.

76
00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:35.000
Treating calibration as a recurring operational task, scheduled alongside model updates and prompt changes,

77
00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:40.000
keeps the routing thresholds honest over time rather than slowly drifting out of alignment.

78
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:44.000
Calibration is what makes a confidence score actionable.

79
00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:48.000
A validation set supplies labeled ground truth.

80
00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:55.000
Binding predictions by score and measuring accuracy reveals where the model is over- or under-confident,

81
00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:00.000
and the reliability diagram makes those gaps visible enough to set a threshold against.

82
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.000
Because a model upgrade or a prompt revision shifts the score distribution,

83
00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:10.000
this is recurring work rather than a one-time setup, so keep that in mind.

84
00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:14.000
With that, we'll wrap up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

